course: everyday
lesson: 495
title: Cold parents: always on the phone
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In Brighton, Avery runs in with a gold star. «Mum, look!» «Mm-hm,» says Mum, eyes on her screen. «Mum, you're not listening.» «I am, babe.» She isn't. Avery feels invisible. Some parents are glued to their phones, for work or out of habit, and they often don't notice what it feels like. A clear ask often works: «Can you put your phone down for one minute?» In Cardiff, Lowri's mam works from home and says, «Not now, cariad, I'm on a call» («cariad» is Welsh for love) every single day, so Lowri stops asking. A plan works better than «now»: «Can we play after your last call?»
explain: Some parents are cool or distant: they say little and rarely praise or hug. In Seattle, Bertie wins a science prize; Dad says, «Cool,» and goes back to his game. «It's giving zero interest,» Bertie mutters («it's giving» means it looks like). In Norwich, Sid watches his friend's dad hug him after a match and wishes his own dad would. It's often about the parent's own stress or how they were brought up, not about the child. Sid can ask for a hug, and everyone can share their wins with other grown-ups too: a teacher, a grandparent, a coach. You deserve to be noticed.
explain: In Essex, dinner is Mum on Instagram, Dad on emails and Harvey on YouTube, and nobody speaks. It feels normal, but empty. Families can drift apart through screens without noticing, and anyone can suggest «no phones at dinner», as long as it's for everyone, Harvey included. In London, Rosa's dad picks her up for the weekend after the divorce, says «hola» and stares at his phone till Sunday. She feels like a parcel being collected. It's not her fault; some parents struggle after a divorce, and she can tell her mum or a trusted adult how weekends feel.
explain: When home is cold, children look for warmth elsewhere, and that's healthy. In Leeds, Juno tells her teacher, «Mum's busy,» about parents' evening, though she knows Mum just didn't want to come; she can tell her teacher how she feels. In Brisbane, Ty's mum never asks about his day, so after school, every arvo («arvo» is Aussie for afternoon), he tells his teacher everything instead. In Dundee, Callie gets hurt at school and her mam says, «Ye're fine, stop greetin'» («greetin'» is Scots for crying) and goes back to the phone. If you're hurt, tell a teacher or another grown-up: your feelings matter.
explain: Not every quiet parent is cold. In Edinburgh, Otto's dad hardly talks, but every Sunday he takes Otto fishing by the loch (Scots for lake). They sit for hours and say almost nothing, and Otto thinks Dad doesn't care. Then, packing up, Dad says, «Best day of my week, this.» Some people show love by sharing time, not words. Notice the quiet ways people care, and if you're unsure, you can ask.
fact: Some parents are glued to their phones for work or habit, and it can make children feel unimportant.
fact: A clear, calm ask like «Can you put your phone down for one minute?» often works.
fact: Asking for a set time, like «after your last call», works better than asking for «now».
fact: Some parents are cold or distant because of their own stress or upbringing; it is not the child's fault.
fact: Some parents show love by doing things or sharing time, not by hugs or words.
fact: Children can share their wins with other grown-ups, like a teacher, a grandparent or a coach.
fact: You can ask a parent for a hug, and look for warmth from other people too.
fact: Families can drift apart through screens without noticing; «no phones at dinner» works best when it's for everyone.
fact: Some parents struggle after a divorce and withdraw; it is not the child's fault, and the child can tell the other parent or a trusted adult.
fact: Finding other grown-ups who listen, like a teacher, is healthy.
fact: If you're hurt or upset and a parent brushes it off, tell a teacher or another grown-up; your feelings matter.
fact: If you're not sure whether a quiet parent cares, you can notice what they do and you can ask.
fact: Words in this lesson: «cariad» (Welsh) means love, «it's giving» means it looks like, «arvo» (Australia) means afternoon, «greetin'» (Scotland) means crying, «loch» (Scotland) means lake, «hola» is Spanish for hello, «Mm-hm» is a sound people make when only half listening.
exercise: Avery said, «Mum, look!» What did Mum say, eyes on the screen? | «Mm-hm» | «Well done, love!» | «Show me later, babe» | «Brilliant, a gold star!»
exercise: How did Avery feel? | Invisible | Proud of Mum | Sleepy | Excited
exercise: What clear ask could Avery try? | «Can you put your phone down for one minute?» | «Give me your phone or I'll scream really loud» | «I'm going to throw your phone in the bath» | «Why don't you ever love me at all, Mum?»
exercise: Lowri's mam always says «Not now, I'm on a call». What could work better? | Asking for a set time, after her last call | Asking her every two minutes until she says yes | Unplugging the computer during the call | Never asking her to play ever again
exercise: «Not now, cariad.» What does «cariad» mean? | Love | Carrot | Car | Card
exercise: Bertie won a science prize and Dad said «Cool». Whose fault is Dad's coldness? | Not Bertie's | Bertie's | The prize's | The game's
exercise: «It's giving zero interest.» What does «it's giving» mean? | It looks like | It's handing out | It's taking away | It's not like
exercise: Who else could Bertie share his prize with? | A teacher, grandparent or coach | Nobody, prizes are private | Only Dad, again and again | Strangers on the internet
exercise: Sid wishes his dad would hug him. What can Sid do? | Ask for one, and find warmth from others too | Wait in silence for years until it happens | Hug his friend's dad instead after every match | Stop going to matches so he doesn't see hugs
exercise: What's the fair rule for dinner at Harvey's? | No phones for anyone, him too | No phones for Mum and Dad only | Phones only for the kids | Phones only on the weekends
exercise: Rosa's dad stares at his phone all weekend. Why does she feel like «a parcel»? | She's collected but not talked to | She's wrapped in brown paper | Her dad works for the post office | She gets sent to the wrong house
exercise: Whose fault is Rosa's dad withdrawing after the divorce? | Not Rosa's | Rosa's | Her mum's friends' | Her teacher's
exercise: Juno says Mum is «busy», but knows she didn't want to come. Who can she talk to? | Her teacher | Nobody | The other mums | A shop assistant
exercise: Ty tells his teacher everything instead of his mum. Is that OK? | Yes, finding a grown-up who listens is healthy | No, he must only ever talk to his mum | No, teachers aren't allowed to listen | Yes, but only if he tells no one else
exercise: «Ye're fine, stop greetin'.» What is Callie's mam telling her to stop? | Crying | Greeting people | Eating | Talking
exercise: What should Callie do if she's hurt and Mam brushes it off? | Tell a teacher or another grown-up | Keep it to herself and hope it heals | Shout at Mam until she puts the phone down | Hide the hurt so she doesn't annoy anyone
exercise: What did Otto's dad say by the loch? | «Best day of my week, this» | «Can we go home yet, son?» | «This is so boring, Otto» | «Fishing's a waste of time»
exercise: What does Otto's story teach? | Some people show love by sharing time | Quiet dads never care about anybody | Fishing is the best way to talk loads | You should never go fishing with dads
test: «if ur mum's always on her phone she doesn't love u». True? | Not necessarily, it's often habit or work | Yes, phones always mean no love at all | Yes, mums can't love phones and kids both | No, it means she loves you the most ever
test: Harvey: «no phones at dinner for u two, but I'm keeping mine». What will probably happen? | Mum and Dad say it's not fair, and it fails | Mum and Dad happily agree straight away | Everyone stops using phones forever | Harvey gets a second phone as a reward
test: Otto thinks Dad doesn't care because he hardly speaks. Which clue in the story shows otherwise? | Every Sunday he takes Otto fishing | He gives Otto a big hug every night | He talks to Otto for hours on end | He buys Otto lots of new toys
test: In a film, the kid fixes a cold dad by winning a big prize, and the dad cries with pride. What's more real? | A distant parent's coldness usually isn't about prizes | Winning a prize always makes cold dads warm | Cold dads only care about trophies and cups | Kids must win prizes so their dads love them
test: Lowri asked to play at 4, at 5 and at 6, and each time Mam was on a call. Her last call ends at 6.30. When is the smartest time to ask? | Just after 6.30 | At 4 again tomorrow | During the next call | Never, give up
test: Ty: «reckon my teacher knows more about me than my mum, is that weird?» («reckon» means think.) What's true? | It's healthy to have a grown-up who listens | It's weird, so he should stop talking to her | It's a secret he must never tell anyone | It means his teacher is now his real mum
test: Callie's mate: «my mam said «stop greetin'» too, that's normal, just get on with it». Callie's arm is still sore after an hour. What should she do? | Tell a teacher or another grown-up | Get on with it and hope it stops | Ask her mate to look at it later | Go back to class and say nothing
test: What would most likely happen if Avery never told Mum how the phone made her feel? | Mum might never notice | Mum would read her mind | The phone would break by itself | Avery would stop caring
test: Rosa's dad says «hola» on Friday at 6, and the next thing he says to her is «adiós» on Sunday at 6. How long is that with almost no talk? | 48 hours | 24 hours | 36 hours | 12 hours
test: Bertie's prize, Avery's gold star, Sid's goal: what's one thing all three kids could do this week? | Share it with another grown-up who'll notice | Stop trying, since nobody at home notices | Win something bigger so parents look up | Throw their parents' phones in the bin
test: Sid: «my dad's never hugged me, so he must hate me». Which is a fairer thought? | Some dads find it hard to show love | All dads who don't hug hate their kids | Hugs are the only way to show love | Sid should hate his dad back for it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the coldness of certain parents? | It frequently arises from the parent's own stress or upbringing | It is invariably caused by some fault on the part of the child | It is a punishment for winning prizes in the field of science | It arises only in households where there are no telephones
exam: Identify the request most likely to secure the attention of a parent preoccupied with a telephone. | A calm request to set it aside for one minute | A threat to dispose of the telephone in water | A repetition of the request every two minutes | A silent withdrawal from the parent's presence
exam: Given that a child is hurt and the parent dismisses the injury, ascertain the most appropriate course. | To inform a teacher or another adult | To conceal the injury from all persons | To await recovery without remark | To reproach the parent at length
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning parents who seldom speak. | Some express love through shared time rather than words | All of them are indifferent to the welfare of their children | They speak seldom because their children are uninteresting | They are prohibited from speaking whilst engaged in fishing
